  "query_preview": "..The upper air pattern remains in a transitional\nphase this week with high pressure centered over the southeast \nU.S. and troughing over the west coast. After an active weekend \nand beginning of the week monsoon activity will decrease over \nthe next couple of days. Southwesterly flow in the mid-levels \nwill reduce moisture slightly today and Wednesday. Enough \nmoisture will still be in place for isolated thunderstorms south\nand east of Tucson each afternoon and evening. CAMs show \nWednesday as being the slightly more active day with storms \ncapable of gusty outflow winds and brief heavy rainfall mainly \nalong the international border. \n\nLate in the week into the weekend the tropics will begin to come\ninto play along with a deepening trough over the Pacific\nNorthwest. Just named tropical storm Marie, currently centered\nwell south of Baja, will be moving northwestward over the next\nfew days. The NHC track keeps it well west of Baja as it travels\nnorthward, but there is some uncertainty in the track after\nFriday. Some models favor a more northward track rather then\nnorthwestward. This will depend on how far south the\naforementioned upper level low travels. However even with the\nuncertainty in the track ensembles are in fairly good agreement\nwith an increase in moisture from the tropics beginning as early\nas Thursday. Expect monsoon thunderstorm activity to ramp back\nup starting Thursday into the weekend. By Friday mean PWAT\nvalues are 1.5\"-1.8\" from Tucson westward. PWAT values near 2\"\nin far western Pima County are not out of the question. This\nincrease in moisture will lead to a risk for heavy rainfall and\nflash flooding. WPC already has southeast Arizona under a slight\nrisk for excessive rainfall Thursday and Friday. \n\nHigh temperatures will remain a few degrees below normal through\nthe rest of the work week then warm a few degrees to around\nnormal this weekend.",
